    2	name: Session numbering vs .run_state
    3	description: In prime-research, .run_state tracks run.sh invocations (lags), while session NN files in archive/sessions/ are numbered consecutively across runs — pick max(existing) + 1.
    4	type: project
    5	originSessionId: c8940663-c50a-48f3-8bae-bcdf41d8e227
    6	---
    7	`.run_state` and session NN are DIFFERENT counters.
    8	
    9	- `.run_state` increments per `./run.sh` invocation.
    10	- `archive/sessions/sessionNN_*.md` files are numbered consecutively
    11	  across all runs (one run can produce multiple sessions via the
    12	  rotation: novelty / construction / arc / lean / critique / verify).
    13	- The two counters drift apart because most runs produce 1+ session.
    14	
    15	**Why:** Why this matters: when the prompt says "echo 130 > .run_state",
    16	that is the next `run.sh` increment, NOT the next session number. The
    17	session number must be `max(existing sessionNN files) + 1`.
    18	
    19	**How to apply:** Before writing `archive/sessions/sessionNN_*.md`, do
    20	`ls archive/sessions/ | grep -oE 'session[0-9]+' | sort -V | tail -1` to
    21	get the most recent session number, then increment. Use the same NN
    22	inside the file (titles, references like "S<NN>") AND in EDGES.md
